encyclopedia :sureni do
  name "Sureni"
  summary "Sureni are coastal plants of Korvath whose buoyant fronds lift their roots from soft ground as the tide rises, allowing whole beds to drift and settle elsewhere."
  kind :lifeform
  subkind :plant
  status :complete
  topics :ecology, :household, :subject_planetary_life
  prevalence :common
  appears_when all: { place: [:outer_system, :waterway] }
  biology "Salt-tolerant floating fronds joined to a flexible root basket, with gas bladders that lift the plant at high water"
  principal_accommodation "Sheltered tidal water, light and soft ground within reach of the roots"
  descriptive_identity appearance: "Round green bladders rise among bronze ribbon leaves above a hanging basket of pale roots.", behavior: "High water lifts a lightly rooted bed; the falling tide presses its root basket into a new patch of mud.", risks: "A drifting bed can hide a channel edge or catch a small vessel against a fixed obstruction."
  prose <<~PROSE
    Sureni beds line quiet reaches of #{ref :istrava}'s coast. Their bronze leaves spread over gas-filled bladders, while flexible root baskets catch mud beneath them. Rising water lifts a recently rooted bed free. Wind and tide carry it until the water falls and the basket settles again. Old beds with deep tangled roots may stay in place through many tides, growing floating margins that break away separately.

    Around #{ref :kethra}, young fish shelter among the roots and children search the stranded leaves for empty shells. Residents tow loose beds away from entrances before they settle across a shallow approach. A bed can conceal a swimmer from someone standing above it, but its roots catch straps and its bladders burst loudly under a boot. The #{ref :ilvaren, "Ilvaren"} use sureni fringes to shelter recovering coastal animals, leaving an open channel through which larger occupants can depart.

    Growers beside #{ref :oskara} harvest young leaf stalks for food and dry mature ribbons for basketwork. Beds moved into still ornamental pools need their accumulated mud thinned by hand. Without the tide's lifting and settling, roots can choke the pool. A sureni mat carried elsewhere brings its small inhabitants along, which pleases a collector and worries a keeper whose pond already has a different community.
  PROSE
  cue "A familiar green bank drifts sideways, revealing open water where yesterday's path ended."
  cue "A boot bursts a round bladder with a sharp report and drops through the bronze leaves."
  affordance "A floating bed provides concealment and shelter for small aquatic life, and can be towed into a temporary protected margin."
  variation "Young beds drift readily and can be moved by a few people with a boat."
  variation "Old rooted beds hold firm while their loose edges travel, leaving channels that shift around an apparently stable center."
end
